  "image_name": "2b3e676357118d0a406c94a68f7a2e09.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways marine protists obtain photosynthetic capacity or nutrients from other organisms. Overall, it contrasts true symbiosis, mixotrophy, and kleptoplasty across planktonic and benthic microbes.\n\nAt the top, free-living photosynthetic dinoflagellates are shown becoming associated with a planktonic foraminifer, where they appear to live attached among the host’s spines as in hospite symbionts. This highlights a host–symbiont relationship in which a non-photosynthetic protist carries photosynthetic partners.\n\nThe middle section presents mixotrophy as a continuum between phototrophic and phagotrophic lifestyles. Different flagellates are positioned along this gradient, suggesting that some rely mainly on light but supplement growth with prey-derived macronutrients, whereas others depend more strongly on prey for trace elements, vitamins, carbon, energy, and nutrients.\n\nThe lower section illustrates kleptoplasty and serial acquisition of plastids. In one pathway, a cryptophyte is eaten by the ciliate Mesodinium rubrum, which then serves as prey for Dinophysis, transferring functional plastids in a secondary kleptoplasty sequence. Another example shows a benthic foraminifer retaining plastids from diatom prey. Together, the figure emphasizes diverse strategies by which protists exploit prey or symbionts for photosynthesis and nutrition.",
